       object (_o_b_j___r_a_w) or an object abstraction (_o_b_j_e_c_t___a_b_s_t_r_a_c_t, see
       pprroovviiddeerr--oobbjjeecctt(7)) into an encoded form, and write the result to the
       given OSSL_CORE_BIO.  If the caller wants to get the encoded stream to
       memory, it should provide a BBIIOO__ss__mmeemm(3) BBIIOO.

       The encoder doesn’t need to know more about the OOSSSSLL__CCOORREE__BBIIOO pointer
       than being able to pass it to the appropriate BIO upcalls (see "Core
       functions" in pprroovviiddeerr--bbaassee(7)).

       The ENCODER implementation may be part of a chain, where data is passed
       from one to the next.  For example, there may be an implementation to
       encode an object to DER (that object is assumed to be provider‐native
       and thereby passed via _o_b_j___r_a_w), and another one that encodes DER to
       PEM (that one would receive the DER encoding via _o_b_j___a_b_s_t_r_a_c_t).

       The encoding using the OOSSSSLL__PPAARRAAMM(3) array form allows a encoder to be
       used for data that’s been exported from another provider, and thereby
       allow them to exist independently of each other.

       The encoding using a provider side object can only be safely used with
       provider data coming from the same provider, for example keys with the
       KEYMGMT provider.

